course: dictionary
lesson: 10392
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and trial is one of them. You can work out which meaning is right by reading the words around it. «A jury is twelve ordinary adults picked at random who listen to a criminal trial and decide whether the person is guilty or not guilty.» The clues are jury, guilty and criminal, so here a trial is a court case.
explain: Now read this: «It is a funny, made-up trial, but every piece of it comes from real history.» The clue is made-up, so this trial is a pretend court case, not a real one. Try your meaning in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right one.
explain: Trial can also mean a test. In a story, the King says, «The trial cannot proceed until all the jurymen are back in their proper places», and that is a court trial. But when you read about a free trial of an app, a trial is a test to see how good, useful or safe something is. Always check the clues before you choose.
fact: Trial is a noun, and its plural is trials.
fact: A trial can be a court case, where a judge and often a jury decide if someone is guilty of a crime.
fact: A trial can also be a test to see how good, useful or safe something is, or a try-out for a sports team.
fact: «Trial and error» means trying different ways until one works.
fact: In maths, «trial and improvement» means guessing, checking and getting closer to the answer.
exercise: «The jury listened carefully to the trial, then gave its decision.» What does trial mean here? | A test of how safe a new toy is | *A court case where a judge and jury decide guilt | Something annoying you have to put up with | A try-out for a sports team
exercise: Sam says, «The new bike was on trial for a week before we bought it.» What does trial mean? | A sports match | A court case | *A test to see how good it is | Something hard to put up with
exercise: What does «trial and error» mean? | Stopping a test early | Running very fast in a race | *Trying different ways until one works | Making the same mistake again and again
test: «Mia had a trial for the football team on Saturday.» What does trial mean? | A difficult lesson | *A try-out to see if she is good enough | A science test of a new recipe | A court case
test: «The long wait for the bus was a real trial.» What does trial mean? | *Something difficult or annoying to put up with | A court case | A try-out for a team | A test of how safe the bus is
exam: Which word in «It is a funny, made-up trial, but every piece of it comes from real history.» tells you the trial is pretend? | *made-up | history | real | piece
